From 2b4f1ac0fcca77e700ce5a0ed5c3e78f9d13a028 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:43:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/12] Start every WAL with a copy of the data header
 (decisions 11 and 14)

The first batch that extends an empty WAL writes a header frame at frame
0 in both modes: the data header its frames depend on, with a marker
recovery skips. An open restores a data header a power loss dropped
(empty, short, never written back or torn) from it, writable or read-only,
and never initializes a new database over the WAL of one whose data file
lost its header: it restores it or refuses, changing neither file. Before,
such a WAL was silently discarded (durable commits=false on storage that
cannot sync, then a power loss).

Commits no longer depend on the data header being on the device, so the
"header not proven" refusal goes and log syncs no longer wait for it. A
best-effort data barrier stays before an engine's first durable commit
(once per process and header; once per shared connection), so a file
someone copied into place is on the device before commits build on it.

Also: a batch without pages runs no proof (a no-op update on a log that
cannot sync neither throws nor records a failure); only a log the engine
opens is remembered as proven; the proven-log cache forgets its oldest
path, never all of them; the rebuild reader restores a lost header too.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
